Indigo's change: AESND_Reset lets the DSP finish before it stops it.

AESND_Reset turned interrupts off and mailed the DSP to stop at once. But
the mixer takes no mail until the CPU has read the one it sent last, and
the CPU reads it in the DSP interrupt: a frame still being mixed answered,
its answer was never read, and both sides waited for ever. A game's launch
froze until the console was switched off. Everywhere else, AESND mails the
DSP only once __aesnddspcomplete says the last frame is done; the reset now
waits for that too, and for the task to have started (__aesnddspinit),
letting the DSP interrupt in while it waits. Under libogc2's licence
(libogc2/libogc2_license.txt).

--- a/libaesnd/aesndlib.c
+++ b/libaesnd/aesndlib.c
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+/* Altered for Indigo: AESND_Reset lets the DSP finish before it stops it
+ * (cube/swiss/aesnd/0001-reset-waits-for-the-dsp.patch). */
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <string.h>
@@ -467,6 +469,16 @@
 	if(__aesndinit) {
 		AUDIO_StopDMA();
 		AUDIO_RegisterDMACallback(NULL);
+
+		/* The mixer takes no mail until the CPU has read the one it sent
+		 * last (wait_mail_sent, then wait_mail_recv), and the CPU reads it in
+		 * the DSP interrupt, which is off here: a frame still being mixed
+		 * would answer, its answer would never be read, and the stop below
+		 * would never be taken. Let the DSP interrupt see the task started
+		 * and the last frame finish; the DSP then waits for mail, and takes
+		 * the stop at once. */
+		while(!__aesnddspinit || !__aesnddspcomplete)
+			_CPU_ISR_Flash(level);
 
 		DSP_SendMailTo(0xfacedead);
 		while(DSP_CheckMailTo());
